--TEST--
`as string<K>` keeps `tainted` on the text, unlike `as propertyPath<T>`: a `tainted string` converts to a `tainted string<K>`, which a `string<K>` parameter does not accept, and its `as string` is a `tainted string`, which `Core\Db`'s query text does not accept — `rule:types/string-holes`, `rule:security/sink-predicate`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The holes of a condition are checked, but the text around them is still
// the caller's text. So a condition from outside stays tainted.
class Order {
    public int $total = 40;
}

class Query {
    public static function where(string<propertyPath<Order>> $condition): string {
        return $condition as string;
    }
}

tainted string $outside = "{total} > 0 OR 1 = 1";

// A path alone is only a declared name, so it is not tainted.
propertyPath<Order> $path = "total" as propertyPath<Order>;
// A condition is the caller's text with holes in it, so it stays tainted.
var $condition = $outside as string<propertyPath<Order>>;
echo Query::where($condition), "\n";

var $db = Core\Db::connect("main");
var $rows = $db->query($condition as string, []);
--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0401]: expected `string<propertyPath<Order>>`, found `tainted string<propertyPath<Order>>`
   --> %s:20:19
   |
20 | echo Query::where($condition), "\n";
   |                   ^^^^^^^^^^ this is `tainted string<propertyPath<Order>>`

error[E0401]: expected `string`, found `tainted string`
   --> %s:23:24
%A
error: aborting due to 2 errors
